Only one verse in this chapter states a kinship relation. In 9.5.26, Ambarīṣa hands the kingdom to his sons, who are of similar character. The sons are not named, so no named person can serve as "a". Under the rules (no persons the verses do not name), the verse gives a statement only if I can name both parties. I could write Ambarīṣa as the father, but the relation form is "a son_of b", and the sons are unnamed. So there is nothing to list.

The other verses are praise, narration or prayer, and none state a genealogical relation.

